faqs:
- q: What are retirement removals?
  a: 'Retirement removals are moves for people leaving a family home for a retirement village, flat, bungalow or sheltered housing: deciding what fits, careful packing, a fully insured removal and settling in. Chayce provides them from Northampton across the UK, from {bronze}.'
- q: Can you help me move into a retirement village?
  a: Yes. We help you sort, pack, move and settle into a retirement village, retirement apartment or bungalow anywhere in the UK, working to your move-in date.
- q: How do I fit my belongings into a smaller home?
  a: We help you decide what fits, what to pass on and what to sell or donate, so you only move what will work in the new space.
- q: Can my family help from a distance?
  a: Yes. Family can be involved by phone or video, and your coordinator keeps everyone updated.
- q: How much does a move to a retirement home cost?
  a: Packing and removals start at {bronze} and full-service moves at {silver}. A Discovery Consultation at {discovery} gives you a personalised budget first.
- q: Do you recommend retirement villages?
  a: No. We help with the move itself, so the choice of where to live stays with you and your family.
- q: Is the removal insured?
  a: Yes. Every Chayce removal is fully insured against loss or damage in transit, and the cover is set out in your service agreement before the move begins.
- q: Do you work outside Northampton?
  a: Yes. We are based in Northampton and arrange moves across the whole UK.
- q: What is the difference between a retirement village, a retirement flat and sheltered housing?
  a: A retirement village is a community of independent homes, such as flats and bungalows, on one site. A retirement flat is a flat in a building designed or converted for older residents. Sheltered housing is a flat or bungalow in a scheme with shared areas and a scheme manager or support arrangements. See our <a href="/blog/moving-into-a-retirement-village-uk-guide/">guide to retirement housing options</a> and our page on <a href="/moving-into-sheltered-housing/">moving into sheltered housing</a>.
- q: What should I ask before moving into a retirement village?
  a: Ask what the service charge covers and how it can rise, whether a fee is payable when you sell or leave, whether care can be added on site, what the rules are on pets, guests and parking, and how moving in works, including delivery windows and lift booking. Our <a href="/blog/moving-into-a-retirement-village-uk-guide/">guide</a> has a full checklist.
- q: Can you work around delivery windows and lift booking?
  a: Yes. Delivery windows, and lift or parking restrictions, are built into the plan, so the move fits what your new community allows.
- q: Can you help if I am selling my home to move in?
  a: Yes. We can coordinate with your estate agent and solicitor, and storage can bridge a gap between sale completion and move-in. Estate agent and solicitor liaison is part of Gold Prestige, which also includes up to 6 weeks of storage. See <a href="/storage-during-your-move/">storage between moves</a>.
- q: How far ahead should I plan a move into retirement housing?
  a: Ideally three to six months before your intended move date, though we can work to shorter timescales where needed.
- q: Do you help with sheltered housing or assisted living?
  a: Yes. We help with the practical move into sheltered housing, assisted living, a retirement flat or a village. See <a href="/moving-into-sheltered-housing/">moving into sheltered housing</a> and our <a href="/blog/care-home-assisted-living-sheltered-housing-or-staying-put-uk/">comparison of the options</a>. We do not give care advice.
